WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.509
How robust can any classifier possibly be against
an adversary that perturbs inputs at test time?

00:00:06.589 --> 00:00:10.339
This paper answers that question for the
multi-class setting.

00:00:10.419 --> 00:00:15.800
The authors derive achievable information-theoretic
lower bounds on the 0-1 loss of the

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:19.065
best possible classifier under a test-time attacker,

00:00:19.145 --> 00:00:20.634
for any discrete dataset.

00:00:20.714 --> 00:00:26.284
Their framework builds a conflict hypergraph from
the data and the attacker's constraints,

00:00:26.364 --> 00:00:31.809
then solves a linear program whose optimum is the
lowest loss any classifier can attain.

00:00:31.889 --> 00:00:35.198
Because the exact problem is often too large to solve,

00:00:35.278 --> 00:00:39.970
they introduce efficient bounds that pin down the
range of the optimal loss,

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:45.368
and use them to reveal a striking gap between
today's adversarially trained models and

00:00:45.448 --> 00:00:47.440
what is theoretically achievable.

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:53.559
Determining whether a classifier is truly robust to
adversarial examples requires knowing

00:00:53.639 --> 00:00:55.586
the best that is even possible.

00:00:55.666 --> 00:01:00.620
For binary classification, prior work characterized
this optimal robust loss,

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:04.086
giving a reference point to measure progress against.

00:01:04.166 --> 00:01:09.055
But real problems have many classes, and the
multi-class case was left open.

00:01:09.135 --> 00:01:14.678
There was no way to compute the lowest 0-1 loss
achievable by any classifier against a

00:01:14.758 --> 00:01:17.555
test-time attacker on a multi-class dataset,

00:01:17.635 --> 00:01:23.570
so practitioners had no way to know how far current
defenses sit from the theoretical limit.

00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:29.431
Comparing the robustness of the best possible
classifier to what state-of-the-art training

00:01:29.511 --> 00:01:31.532
achieves is a powerful diagnostic.

00:01:31.612 --> 00:01:38.452
It tells you whether the bottleneck is your
training method or a fundamental limit of the data and threat model.

00:01:38.532 --> 00:01:41.294
Past work delivered this only for two classes.

00:01:41.374 --> 00:01:46.113
Moving to many classes is not a trivial extension:
with three or more classes,

00:01:46.193 --> 00:01:51.427
examples can interact in higher-order ways that
binary analysis simply cannot capture,

00:01:51.507 --> 00:01:55.690
and these interactions can change what optimal
robustness looks like.

00:01:56.070 --> 00:01:59.880
The paper makes three contributions.

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:03.770
First,

00:02:03.850 --> 00:02:07.660
it generalizes the conflict-graph framework from
binary to multi-class classification,

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:11.550
showing the optimal 0-1 loss is the solution of a
linear program built on a conflict hypergraph.

00:02:11.630 --> 00:02:15.440
Second, because that exact program can become
computationally prohibitive,

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:19.330
it develops several more efficient bounds, both
lower and upper,

00:02:19.410 --> 00:02:23.220
that bracket the range in which the true optimal
loss must lie.

00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:27.110
Third, it delivers an extensive empirical study,

00:02:27.190 --> 00:02:31.000
giving the first analysis of the gap to optimal
robustness for classifiers in the multi-class

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:34.890
setting on benchmark datasets.

00:02:35.270 --> 00:02:40.396
The core idea is to represent the classification
problem as a conflict hypergraph.

00:02:40.476 --> 00:02:42.174
Each vertex is a data point,

00:02:42.254 --> 00:02:47.570
and a set of points forms a hyperedge when they
belong to different classes yet share

00:02:47.650 --> 00:02:49.983
overlapping adversarial neighborhoods,

00:02:50.063 --> 00:02:54.490
meaning an attacker could push any of them to the
same confusing input.

00:02:54.570 --> 00:03:00.458
The optimal 0-1 loss then becomes a linear program:
maximize the probability mass of correctly

00:03:00.538 --> 00:03:05.918
classified points subject to constraints encoded by
the hypergraph's incidence matrix.

00:03:05.998 --> 00:03:10.235
The dual program is a fractional covering of
vertices by hyperedges,

00:03:10.315 --> 00:03:13.791
and it directly yields the optimal adversarial strategy.

00:03:13.871 --> 00:03:17.854
Because computing all hyperedges up to degree ten
is infeasible,

00:03:17.934 --> 00:03:22.933
the authors truncate the hypergraph to lower
degrees for tractable lower bounds,

00:03:23.013 --> 00:03:27.060
aggregate binary one-versus-one losses for an even
cheaper bound,

00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:31.250
and apply a generalized Caro-Wei bound for a
matching upper bound.

00:03:31.630 --> 00:03:36.280
The experiments use two standard vision benchmarks,
MNIST and CIFAR-10,

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:41.942
under an L-two constrained attacker evaluated
across a sweep of perturbation budgets.

00:03:42.022 --> 00:03:47.471
For the 3-class studies they take one thousand
samples per class, using digits one,

00:03:47.551 --> 00:03:52.600
four, and seven for MNIST and the plane, bird, and
ship classes for CIFAR-10.

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:58.262
They also compute bounds for the full 10-class
problem on the complete training sets.

00:03:58.342 --> 00:04:03.524
As reference defenses, they train classifiers with
TRADES adversarial training,

00:04:03.604 --> 00:04:08.920
a small convolutional network for MNIST and a wide
residual network for CIFAR-10,

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:13.050
and evaluate them with the strong APGD attack from
AutoAttack.

00:04:13.430 --> 00:04:17.355
The headline finding is a large, previously
unquantified gap.

00:04:17.435 --> 00:04:22.673
Adversarially trained classifiers perform far worse
than the theoretical optimum,

00:04:22.753 --> 00:04:28.122
and this gap is much wider than what prior work
observed for binary classification.

00:04:28.202 --> 00:04:29.435
On 3-class CIFAR-10,

00:04:29.515 --> 00:04:35.213
TRADES adversarial training cannot achieve a loss
much better than 0.6 at a perturbation

00:04:35.293 --> 00:04:39.349
strength where the optimal achievable loss is
essentially zero.

00:04:39.429 --> 00:04:40.924
In the 10-class setting,

00:04:41.004 --> 00:04:47.752
the efficient lower and upper bounds sandwich the
optimal loss tightly for the budgets used in practice,

00:04:47.832 --> 00:04:50.772
so the gap is not an artifact of loose bounds.

00:04:50.852 --> 00:04:56.550
This suggests current robust training struggles far
more as the number of classes grows.

00:04:56.930 --> 00:05:00.821
A key ablation examines how much the higher-order
hyperedges actually matter.

00:05:00.901 --> 00:05:04.792
Surprisingly,

00:05:04.872 --> 00:05:08.763
at small perturbation budgets the lower bound
computed with only edges is nearly identical

00:05:08.843 --> 00:05:12.734
to bounds that add degree-three and degree-four
hyperedges,

00:05:12.814 --> 00:05:16.705
even though the graph contains millions of these
higher-order structures.

00:05:16.785 --> 00:05:20.675
For CIFAR-10 at budget three,

00:05:20.755 --> 00:05:24.646
there are roughly three million degree-three and
ten million degree-four hyperedges,

00:05:24.726 --> 00:05:28.617
yet they have no impact on the computed bound.

00:05:28.697 --> 00:05:32.588
This means edge-only bounds are both cheap and
accurate in the practical regime.

00:05:32.668 --> 00:05:36.559
The aggregated binary bound is the fastest to
compute but much looser,

00:05:36.639 --> 00:05:40.530
and scaling up model architecture yields only minor
gains at low budgets.

00:05:40.910 --> 00:05:45.032
The numbers make the gap concrete.

00:05:45.112 --> 00:05:49.233
On 3-class CIFAR-10,

00:05:49.313 --> 00:05:53.435
adversarial training plateaus near 0.6 loss at a
budget where the optimal loss is essentially zero.

00:05:53.515 --> 00:05:57.637
State-of-the-art certifiably robust models fare no
better against the optimum: the best

00:05:57.717 --> 00:06:01.838
MNIST model has 0-1 loss of 0.27 at budget
one-point-five-two and 0.44 at budget two,

00:06:01.918 --> 00:06:06.040
while the achievable optimal lower bound is zero in
both cases.

00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:10.242
On CIFAR-10,

00:06:10.322 --> 00:06:14.443
the best certified model reaches 0.6 loss at budget
one and 0.8 at budget two,

00:06:14.523 --> 00:06:18.645
again against an optimal lower bound of zero.

00:06:18.725 --> 00:06:22.847
And on the structural side, millions of
higher-degree hyperedges,

00:06:22.927 --> 00:06:27.048
three million of degree three and ten million of
degree four at budget three,

00:06:27.128 --> 00:06:31.250
leave the bound completely unchanged.

00:06:31.630 --> 00:06:36.751
The takeaway is that multi-class robust
classification has a large and now-measurable

00:06:36.831 --> 00:06:41.463
gap between what current defenses achieve and what
is theoretically possible,

00:06:41.543 --> 00:06:44.400
a gap that is far worse than in the binary case.

00:06:44.480 --> 00:06:50.029
The paper's conflict-hypergraph framework computes
the optimal 0-1 loss as a linear program,

00:06:50.109 --> 00:06:57.188
and its efficient truncated bounds pin that optimum
down tightly using only edges in the practical low-budget regime.

00:06:57.268 --> 00:07:03.124
This gives practitioners a fast diagnostic tool to
see how much robustness is still on the table,

00:07:03.204 --> 00:07:09.610
and it points future research toward closing the
gap rather than endlessly iterating attacks and defenses.
